ECE4016 DNS server - progress and test record
Date: 2026-10-18 (Asia/Shanghai)

Current implementation (dns_server.py)
- Listens on 127.0.0.1:1234 over UDP. Handles A queries; other query types return NOTIMP.
- FLAG=0 asks public recursive DNS servers (Google, Cloudflare, Quad9, OpenDNS).
- FLAG=1 starts at root DNS servers, follows NS referrals and CNAMEs, and uses
  glue A records or resolves an NS hostname when glue is unavailable.
- Prints the IP of every upstream DNS server contacted.
- Retries a truncated upstream UDP answer over TCP.
- Caches a resolved IPv4 address in memory. The current cache has no expiry;
  restarting the process clears it.
- ValueError messages now explain the failure, and request failures are printed
  to the server console. Malformed client packets do not terminate the server.
- The earlier full-packet debug mode was reverted and is not in this version.

Tests performed on Windows
- Environment: temporary Python 3.9.13 with dnslib 0.9.26. Python 3.9 syntax
  check passed.
- Both FLAG modes were tested by sending real UDP DNS requests to a temporary
  local port. Port 1234 was already occupied by a Python process during this
  controlled test, so that process was left running.
- FLAG=0: www.example.com returned an A record (172.66.147.243 in this run);
  www.baidu.com returned an A record (103.235.47.188 in this run).
- FLAG=1: www.example.com returned an A record (104.20.23.154 in this run);
  www.baidu.com returned an A record (183.2.172.177 in this run). The root
  server's truncated UDP response triggered the TCP retry successfully.
- A second query for each domain returned the same answer without another
  upstream query, showing a cache hit.
- An AAAA query returned NOTIMP. A malformed one-byte request was logged;
  valid requests still succeeded afterward.
- A simulated upstream failure produced a ValueError containing its cause.
- A separate DNS client received NOERROR and an A record from the process on
  127.0.0.1:1234 for www.example.com and www.iana.org. That running process
  may have loaded an earlier revision, so this is only a local-port check.

Windows nslookup finding
- On this computer, nslookup -port=1234 did not test the server on port 1234.
  A temporary packet-capture server observed nslookup sending to port 53 even
  when another port was specified. Its "No response from server" output is
  therefore not evidence that dns_server.py failed or that campus DNS is blocked.
- The independent Python DNS client did receive a valid reply on port 1234.

Recommended final tests on the Linux computer
1. Install the dependency: python3 -m pip install dnslib
2. Set FLAG=0, start the server: python3 dns_server.py
3. In another terminal run:
   dig @127.0.0.1 -p 1234 www.example.com A
   dig @127.0.0.1 -p 1234 www.baidu.com A
4. Repeat each command and verify that no new upstream server IP is printed.
5. Stop the server, set FLAG=1, restart it, and repeat both dig commands.
   Check that the server prints the IPs contacted during iterative lookup.

Still to verify
- The exact dig commands above have not yet been run on Linux.
- DNS access from the Linux computer/campus network has not been checked.
- Answer IPs can change; compare DNS status and presence of an A record,
  rather than expecting the sample IPs above.
